{
  "_comment": "Kelp Watch analysis polygons per docs/03. One Feature per area the analysis compares kelp over. Each declares polygon_id (the key every kelp and comparison row joins on), purpose (near_site, control or regional), site_ids (the stations this polygon is compared against, so no analysis code string-matches a polygon name), and source_file -- the Kelp Watch export its rows arrive in, because the CSV names the geometry it describes nowhere in the file (docs/02). kelp_watch pins the dataset revision the exports came from; bumping it obsoletes every landing at the old revision, because a newer revision may revise history as well as extend it. Geometry is WGS84 (RFC 7946) and is now recorded for all six, as a RECONSTRUCTION rather than a recovered original. Kelp Watch has no named-bed catalogue -- area selection there is draw-on-map or upload-a-file -- so the outlines the operator drew exist in no upstream list and cannot be fetched back. What is recorded instead is derived from Kelp Watch's own classified-cell vector tiles (docs/02, 'The cells behind the export'): every bed is a spatially isolated cluster of 30 m cells, and each outline is that cluster's historic footprint dilated by 90 m and simplified to 40 m. That is not an eyeballed shape -- it selects exactly the cells its landed export was summed over and no others, with at least 49 m of margin inside and 537 m of clearance to the nearest cell of any other bed. Checked against the public /aggregate endpoint on 2026-08-30, all six: count_cells_historic_footprint matches the recorded value and the whole 213-line response diffs clean against the landed CSV, zero differing lines. Nothing in the pipeline computes from these outlines -- the export arrives already summed over the selected geometry -- but docs/04 s4.5's distance-decay test needs them, and that is the stage that should refuse a polygon without one.",
  "_provisional": "The purpose labels and the site pairings below are a first pass and are meant to be revised. They rest on a premise the recorded outlines have now falsified, and correcting the labels is deliberately NOT done here -- see https://github.com/cweber12/kelp-compare/issues/86. The premise was that NDBC:LJAC1's published position (32.867, -117.257) is inside the La Jolla bed, making that bed regional and the other five controls. Measured against the outline recorded here it is 1731 m outside it, to the north, as is SIO:LAJOLLA-PIER at 1718 m: Scripps Pier is north of the La Jolla kelp forest. So no polygon here is regional for the reason this file gives. Every bed is paired with both public reference stations and both project sensors, which is the docs/04 s4.5 comparison; near_site is now declarable and is not yet declared. Both project sites are surveyed in sites.json and both fall inside KELP:LA-JOLLA -- PROJ:TIDBIT-1 is 8.8 m from the centre of a cell that has held canopy, PROJ:TIDBIT-2 is 20 m outside the undilated historic footprint and inside the recorded outline only by its 90 m dilation. What is missing is no longer geometry but the rule: docs/04 s4.5 wants a criterion written down rather than applied once by eye, which is what issue 86 is for. SIO:LAJOLLA-PIER is paired at both of its sensor depths, 0.5 m and 5.0 m, rather than at one chosen here: they are independent series carrying independent coverage bookkeeping, docs/04 s4.1 is exploratory by decision, and whether a surface and a 5 m shore record behave differently against canopy is a question the screen answers rather than one to settle by assertion. It is a daily shore record from a different instrument than LJAC1's hourly hull sensor, so the two sit in one parameter column measuring the same quantity by different means; the screen reports them as separate series and never pools them. Adding a second public reference to all six beds widens the docs/04 s5 denominator, which is accepted: a screen cannot be told it is recovering seasonality by a reference it does not have. Changing any of this is a data(registry) edit needing no code.",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
